  "narrative": "The new validation releases and response deadlines resolve the two open PTEN- and CDKN2A-related items that required immediate attention, while leaving a central dataset-specific association pattern largely unchanged. H212 tested CDKN2A loss within the copy-number 8q24-high subgroup with direction -1, so a positive signed estimate means the raw CDKN2A-loss contrast is negative, i.e. stronger measured dependency. The discovery result was above the 0.15-unit threshold (+0.186, lower bound 0.025) and the newly released independent validation was also positive in direction but very imprecise (+0.174, interval -0.068 to 0.416). Because both point estimates exceed the threshold and run in the same direction, I keep H212 accepted as a supported but imprecise context-specific association. The validation does not establish a precisely bounded effect, and it should not be interpreted as a generalizable biological claim.\n\nThe key new PTEN assessment concerns H226 and H235. H226 was registered as a PTEN-by-TP53-loss interaction in direction -1, meaning the hypothesis was that the raw PTEN-loss contrast would be more negative in TP53-loss backgrounds than in TP53-intact backgrounds. Discovery evidence was instead signed negative (-0.157), corresponding to the opposite raw pattern, and the newly released validation remained signed negative but much smaller and imprecise (-0.072, interval -0.476 to 0.332). Therefore H226 is rejected and closed for its registered direction. This rejection does not establish a meaningful attenuation effect; it mainly says that the originally registered interaction direction is unsupported.\n\nThe reversed attenuation hypothesis is represented by H235, where positive signed estimates correspond to a raw positive interaction and hence attenuation of the PTEN association in TP53-loss backgrounds. H235 has discovery signed evidence +0.157 and validation signed evidence +0.072, but the validation interval includes zero and the point estimate is below the 0.15-unit threshold. Thus H235 remains unresolved and active, not accepted and not rejected. The pattern suggests that the PTEN association may be weaker in TP53-loss backgrounds, especially from the contrast between PTEN simple effects in TP53-intact backgrounds (+0.259) and TP53-loss backgrounds (+0.102), but the current evidence is underpowered and cannot support a precise interaction conclusion.\n\nThe broader supported pattern remains dominated by central measured predictors. KEAP1 loss is strongly and robustly associated with higher dependency_composite values, meaning weaker measured dependency. SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with lower dependency_composite values, meaning stronger measured dependency, and the corresponding direction -1 claims should be read as positive evidence for a raw negative contrast. TP53 loss was initially expected to associate with stronger dependency, but the dataset-specific evidence supports the opposite association: TP53 loss is associated with higher dependency_composite values, i.e. weaker measured dependency. PTEN loss is now an additional accepted dataset-specific predictor in the stronger-dependency direction: PTEN loss is associated with lower dependency_composite values by roughly 0.23 to 0.28 composite units in discovery and validation, though the accepted claim remains imprecise because lower bounds are close to or below the threshold. These associations are dataset-specific and do not establish causality, mechanism, or generalizability.\n\nNegative findings remain broad. The constructed research markers D, E, and F do not show meaningful mean differences. Many global single-gene associations, omics availability indicators, media-serum global contrasts, sex, several CRISPR library and screen-batch contrasts, and numerous central-gene interactions are null-like or unsupported for the registered direction. EGFR amplification remains near-threshold and not sufficiently supported after refinement. Global CDKN2A remains near-threshold and is not accepted; CDKN2A is supported only in selected imprecise copy-number contexts, including the 8q24-high subgroup now confirmed directionally but not precisely by validation.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is to continue probing the dataset-specific PTEN association using adequately powered pairwise contexts rather than pursuing sparse triple-loss estimates. The newly proposed analyses test PTEN loss in TP53- and KEAP1-intact backgrounds, in TP53-loss/KEAP1-intact backgrounds, in TP53-intact/KEAP1-loss backgrounds, and through a PTEN-by-lineage interaction with adenocarcinoma status. These analyses directly address whether the accepted PTEN association persists across the central backgrounds, where the apparent TP53-loss attenuation is coming from, and whether the PTEN association is lineage-scope-specific. If these pairwise contexts remain positive and threshold-exceeding, the PTEN pattern will be more robust. If they remain imprecise or null, the PTEN finding should remain a supported but limited dataset-level association rather than a precisely localized context effect. All remaining conclusions should be phrased as associations in this supplied dataset, not as causal or biologically generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 13 final synthesis. No new validation requests remain; future decisions rely on discovery evidence and existing validations. H212 remains accepted but imprecise: CDKN2A loss in 8q24-high backgrounds has discovery +0.186 and validation +0.174 in direction -1, both threshold-exceeding point estimates, but validation lower bound -0.068 crosses zero. Treat as context-supported imprecise association only. H226 rejected/closed: registered PTEN-by-TP53 interaction direction -1 is unsupported; discovery and validation are signed negative, corresponding to opposite raw interaction. H235 remains unresolved/active: reversed PTEN-by-TP53 attenuation has signed +0.157 discovery and +0.072 validation, below threshold and CI includes zero. Plausible attenuation but not established.\n\nCentral accepted dataset-specific associations: KEAP1 loss higher dependency_composite (weaker measured dependency); SMARCA4 loss lower dependency_composite (stronger measured dependency); TP53 loss higher dependency_composite (weaker dependency, opposite initial expectation); PTEN loss lower dependency_composite (stronger dependency, supported but imprecise after reversal and validation). CDKN2A accepted only in selected copy-number contexts, notably 9p21-deleted and 8q24-high backgrounds, and imprecise. Negative/null: research markers D/E/F, many global single genes, technical/omics availability, global serum, sex, many interactions, EGFR not sufficiently supported, global CDKN2A not accepted.\n\nRegistered next analyses: PTEN simple effects in TP53-intact+KEAP1-intact, TP53-loss+KEAP1-intact, and TP53-intact+KEAP1-loss backgrounds; PTEN-by-adenocarcinoma interaction. These test persistence of PTEN association, localize apparent TP53-loss attenuation, and assess lineage scope without sparse triple-loss estimates. Maintain interpretation as supplied-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable biology."
